1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,233
One hour from now, this city will be

2
00:00:02,243 --> 00:00:02,743
gone.

3
00:00:03,006 --> 00:00:04,318
And nobody here knows it.

4
00:00:04,851 --> 00:00:07,291
Thirty-seven people are arriving for work in

5
00:00:07,301 --> 00:00:09,564
a concrete building beside the river.

6
00:00:09,801 --> 00:00:11,441
In a few minutes, one of them will

7
00:00:11,451 --> 00:00:13,566
walk down to the basement to look for

8
00:00:13,576 --> 00:00:14,514
some papers.

9
00:00:14,979 --> 00:00:15,742
Remember him.

10
00:00:16,005 --> 00:00:18,170
Because what he does next is the difference

11
00:00:18,180 --> 00:00:20,142
between everyone, and one.

12
00:00:20,630 --> 00:00:23,947
It's Monday, August sixth, nineteen forty-five.

13
00:00:24,197 --> 00:00:25,360
You're in Hiroshima.

14
00:00:25,597 --> 00:00:27,272
The sky is perfectly clear.

15
00:00:27,766 --> 00:00:30,769
Today you'll live this morning minute by minute.

16
00:00:31,019 --> 00:00:32,871
And by the end of this video, you'll

17
00:00:32,881 --> 00:00:34,769
know whether you would have survived it.

18
00:00:35,193 --> 00:00:37,424
At nine minutes past seven, the air raid

19
00:00:37,434 --> 00:00:39,184
siren starts to wail.

20
00:00:39,650 --> 00:00:41,111
You've heard it a hundred times.

21
00:00:41,361 --> 00:00:42,399
Tokyo has burned.

22
00:00:42,649 --> 00:00:43,861
Osaka has burned.

23
00:00:44,111 --> 00:00:46,461
Sixty Japanese cities have been firebombed.

24
00:00:46,957 --> 00:00:48,348
But not Hiroshima.

25
00:00:48,648 --> 00:00:50,925
For some reason, the bombers always pass it

26
00:00:50,935 --> 00:00:51,448
by.

27
00:00:51,710 --> 00:00:53,423
People here have started to wonder why.

28
00:00:53,943 --> 00:00:56,952
High above, a single silver plane crosses the

29
00:00:56,962 --> 00:00:57,612
sky.

30
00:00:57,862 --> 00:00:58,825
It drops nothing.

31
00:00:59,087 --> 00:01:01,490
It's a weather plane, checking the clouds over

32
00:01:01,500 --> 00:01:02,150
the city.

33
00:01:02,649 --> 00:01:03,851
And the sky is clear.

34
00:01:04,101 --> 00:01:06,516
That report has just sealed the fate of

35
00:01:06,526 --> 00:01:07,564
everyone around you.

36
00:01:08,070 --> 00:01:11,036
At seven thirty-one, the all-clear sounds.

37
00:01:11,298 --> 00:01:14,251
People climb out of the shelters, stretch, and

38
00:01:14,261 --> 00:01:15,723
go back to their morning.

39
00:01:16,160 --> 00:01:17,292
Breakfast is small.

40
00:01:17,542 --> 00:01:19,857
Rice mixed with barley, maybe a little pickled

41
00:01:19,867 --> 00:01:20,605
radish.

42
00:01:20,867 --> 00:01:22,992
The war has made everyone thin.

43
00:01:23,504 --> 00:01:25,728
By eight o'clock, around three hundred and fifty

44
00:01:25,738 --> 00:01:27,763
thousand people are in Hiroshima.

45
00:01:28,255 --> 00:01:30,844
Soldiers do their morning exercises on the parade

46
00:01:30,854 --> 00:01:32,442
ground near the castle.

47
00:01:32,919 --> 00:01:35,662
Streetcars rattle through the center, packed

48
00:01:35,672 --> 00:01:36,622
with commuters.

49
00:01:36,872 --> 00:01:39,462
Many of the drivers are teenage girls, because

50
00:01:39,472 --> 00:01:40,909
the men are at war.

51
00:01:41,395 --> 00:01:43,863
And in the streets, thousands of schoolchildren

52
00:01:43,873 --> 00:01:45,035
are already at work.

53
00:01:45,514 --> 00:01:48,320
They're twelve, thirteen, fourteen years old.

54
00:01:48,583 --> 00:01:50,948
Their job is to tear down houses, to

55
00:01:50,958 --> 00:01:53,248
make wide gaps that will stop fires from

56
00:01:53,258 --> 00:01:55,395
spreading when the bombers finally come.

57
00:01:55,894 --> 00:01:57,980
More than nine thousand students are doing this

58
00:01:57,990 --> 00:01:58,602
today.

59
00:01:58,827 --> 00:01:59,640
Outdoors.

60
00:01:59,890 --> 00:02:01,040
In short sleeves.

61
00:02:01,277 --> 00:02:02,490
Under an open sky.

62
00:02:03,010 --> 00:02:04,817
In the concrete building by the river, the

63
00:02:04,827 --> 00:02:06,777
clerk you met is at his desk.

64
00:02:07,040 --> 00:02:08,890
His name is Eizo Nomura.

65
00:02:09,152 --> 00:02:10,165
He's forty-seven.

66
00:02:10,679 --> 00:02:12,702
He notices that some documents are missing.

67
00:02:12,952 --> 00:02:14,414
They must be downstairs.

68
00:02:14,677 --> 00:02:15,389
He stands up.

69
00:02:15,845 --> 00:02:18,917
Three kilometers away, a young ship engineer

70
00:02:19,004 --> 00:02:22,617
named Tsutomu Yamaguchi is walking to the shipyard.

71
00:02:22,854 --> 00:02:24,829
It's the last day of his business trip.

72
00:02:25,092 --> 00:02:27,592
Tomorrow, he goes home to Nagasaki.

73
00:02:28,083 --> 00:02:30,586
Now, here's your choice, and you don't even

74
00:02:30,596 --> 00:02:31,546
know you're making it.

75
00:02:31,783 --> 00:02:33,483
Where are you standing at eight fifteen?

76
00:02:33,746 --> 00:02:35,096
Outside, or inside?

77
00:02:35,346 --> 00:02:36,408
Wood, or concrete?

78
00:02:36,658 --> 00:02:38,371
Near the center, or far?

79
00:02:38,868 --> 00:02:39,913
A few people look up.

80
00:02:40,238 --> 00:02:41,688
Three planes, very high.

81
00:02:41,938 --> 00:02:43,138
No siren this time.

82
00:02:43,476 --> 00:02:45,013
Three planes aren't a raid.

83
00:02:45,522 --> 00:02:47,508
One of them is called the Enola Gay.

84
00:02:47,771 --> 00:02:49,746
It took off from the island of Tinian,

85
00:02:49,871 --> 00:02:50,971
six hours ago.

86
00:02:51,479 --> 00:02:53,772
At eight fifteen, a single bomb falls from

87
00:02:53,782 --> 00:02:54,095
it.

88
00:02:54,345 --> 00:02:56,807
It drops for forty-three seconds.

89
00:02:57,283 --> 00:02:59,101
It's aimed at a T-shaped bridge in the

90
00:02:59,111 --> 00:03:00,173
heart of the city.

91
00:03:00,423 --> 00:03:03,151
It drifts a little, and bursts about six

92
00:03:03,161 --> 00:03:05,711
hundred meters above a small hospital instead.

93
00:03:06,204 --> 00:03:07,241
First comes the light.

94
00:03:07,491 --> 00:03:10,506
A silent flash, brighter than anything you have

95
00:03:10,516 --> 00:03:11,229
ever seen.

96
00:03:11,479 --> 00:03:13,016
Survivors will call it pika.

97
00:03:13,279 --> 00:03:14,141
The flash.

98
00:03:14,644 --> 00:03:15,557
With it comes heat.

99
00:03:15,857 --> 00:03:18,022
For a few seconds, the ground beneath the

100
00:03:18,032 --> 00:03:20,532
fireball is hotter than molten iron.

101
00:03:20,997 --> 00:03:22,603
People as far as three and a half

102
00:03:22,613 --> 00:03:25,063
kilometers away feel their skin burn.

103
00:03:25,561 --> 00:03:26,466
Then the blast.

104
00:03:26,728 --> 00:03:29,341
A wall of air moving faster than sound.

105
00:03:29,591 --> 00:03:32,031
Almost every building within two kilometers

106
00:03:32,041 --> 00:03:34,116
is flattened or set on fire.

107
00:03:34,591 --> 00:03:36,398
Wooden houses simply fold.

108
00:03:36,660 --> 00:03:37,960
And then, darkness.

109
00:03:38,223 --> 00:03:40,588
The dust is so thick that morning turns

110
00:03:40,598 --> 00:03:41,185
to night.

111
00:03:41,635 --> 00:03:44,304
Above the city, a cloud climbs higher and

112
00:03:44,314 --> 00:03:45,851
higher into the sky.

113
00:03:46,366 --> 00:03:49,270
Eizo Nomura is in the basement, reaching for

114
00:03:49,280 --> 00:03:51,555
his documents, when the lights go out.

115
00:03:52,041 --> 00:03:53,536
He hears a huge roar.

116
00:03:53,799 --> 00:03:54,874
Something hits his head.

117
00:03:55,136 --> 00:03:56,764
He's sure the building has taken a direct

118
00:03:56,774 --> 00:03:57,199
hit.

119
00:03:57,695 --> 00:03:59,434
He is one hundred and seventy meters from

120
00:03:59,444 --> 00:04:00,907
the center of the explosion.

121
00:04:01,157 --> 00:04:03,409
Closer than any other person who will live

122
00:04:03,419 --> 00:04:04,332
to tell about it.

123
00:04:04,828 --> 00:04:06,351
He feels his way up the stairs in

124
00:04:06,361 --> 00:04:06,986
the dark.

125
00:04:07,249 --> 00:04:09,889
The world he walks out into is not

126
00:04:09,899 --> 00:04:10,849
the one he left.

127
00:04:11,350 --> 00:04:12,677
The whole neighborhood is gone.

128
00:04:12,902 --> 00:04:14,665
Shops, houses, temples.

129
00:04:15,015 --> 00:04:17,040
Fire is rising everywhere.

130
00:04:17,544 --> 00:04:20,196
Thirty-seven people came to work in his building

131
00:04:20,206 --> 00:04:20,906
this morning.

132
00:04:21,181 --> 00:04:23,044
He is the only one who survives.

133
00:04:23,533 --> 00:04:25,950
The only difference between him and the others

134
00:04:25,960 --> 00:04:27,797
was one floor of concrete.

135
00:04:28,294 --> 00:04:29,997
If you're still alive, you have a new

136
00:04:30,007 --> 00:04:30,682
problem.

137
00:04:30,945 --> 00:04:32,670
The city is catching fire.

138
00:04:32,932 --> 00:04:34,257
All of it, all at once.

139
00:04:34,733 --> 00:04:37,445
Thousands of small fires join into one.

140
00:04:37,695 --> 00:04:40,708
The wind begins to blow toward the center,

141
00:04:40,920 --> 00:04:41,708
feeding it.

142
00:04:42,201 --> 00:04:43,578
So here is the next choice.

143
00:04:43,816 --> 00:04:46,218
You can stay and search the ruins for

144
00:04:46,228 --> 00:04:46,978
your family.

145
00:04:47,241 --> 00:04:49,568
Or you can walk away, right now, toward

146
00:04:49,578 --> 00:04:50,416
the hills.

147
00:04:50,889 --> 00:04:51,652
Many stay.

148
00:04:51,915 --> 00:04:52,465
They dig.

149
00:04:52,715 --> 00:04:53,840
They call out names.

150
00:04:54,090 --> 00:04:56,015
And the fire closes in around them.

151
00:04:56,508 --> 00:04:58,149
The ones who live are mostly the ones

152
00:04:58,159 --> 00:04:58,821
who move.

153
00:04:59,059 --> 00:05:00,221
Out toward the edges.

154
00:05:00,484 --> 00:05:03,034
Toward the rivers, the parks, the hills.

155
00:05:03,515 --> 00:05:06,742
Hiroshima is built on seven rivers.

156
00:05:07,067 --> 00:05:09,907
Thousands crowd onto the banks and into the

157
00:05:09,917 --> 00:05:11,955
water, to escape the heat.

158
00:05:12,446 --> 00:05:14,546
Then, about half an hour after the flash,

159
00:05:14,556 --> 00:05:15,606
it begins to rain.

160
00:05:15,894 --> 00:05:17,756
Thick, dark, heavy drops.

161
00:05:18,006 --> 00:05:18,756
Black rain.

162
00:05:19,271 --> 00:05:21,012
People are desperately thirsty.

163
00:05:21,224 --> 00:05:23,074
Some open their mouths to the sky.

164
00:05:23,590 --> 00:05:25,898
Nobody knows that the rain is carrying soot,

165
00:05:26,311 --> 00:05:29,176
ash, and radioactive dust back down on top

166
00:05:29,186 --> 00:05:29,586
of them.

167
00:05:30,091 --> 00:05:33,318
Three kilometers from the center, Tsutomu Yamaguchi

168
00:05:33,493 --> 00:05:36,121
sees the plane, and a small shape falling

169
00:05:36,131 --> 00:05:37,368
beneath a parachute.

170
00:05:37,863 --> 00:05:39,248
Then the sky turns white.

171
00:05:39,511 --> 00:05:41,313
He dives into a ditch, the way he

172
00:05:41,323 --> 00:05:42,123
was trained.

173
00:05:42,616 --> 00:05:44,821
The blast lifts him off the ground and

174
00:05:44,831 --> 00:05:46,306
throws him into the field.

175
00:05:46,568 --> 00:05:48,946
When he opens his eyes, it's dark, and

176
00:05:48,956 --> 00:05:50,643
his arms and face are burned.

177
00:05:51,130 --> 00:05:51,993
But he's alive.

178
00:05:52,256 --> 00:05:53,318
He was far enough.

179
00:05:53,581 --> 00:05:55,683
He spends the night in a shelter, and

180
00:05:55,693 --> 00:05:57,546
the next morning he does the only thing

181
00:05:57,556 --> 00:05:58,568
he can think of.

182
00:05:58,843 --> 00:05:59,618
He goes home.

183
00:06:00,132 --> 00:06:01,726
Home is Nagasaki.

184
00:06:01,989 --> 00:06:03,526
He arrives on August eighth.

185
00:06:03,776 --> 00:06:06,066
On the morning of the ninth, bandaged, he

186
00:06:06,076 --> 00:06:07,564
reports to his office.

187
00:06:08,065 --> 00:06:10,136
He's telling his boss that a single bomb

188
00:06:10,149 --> 00:06:12,011
destroyed an entire city.

189
00:06:12,274 --> 00:06:14,124
His boss tells him that's impossible.

190
00:06:14,604 --> 00:06:16,736
And at that moment, the window behind them

191
00:06:16,746 --> 00:06:18,371
fills with white light.

192
00:06:18,857 --> 00:06:21,494
Tsutomu Yamaguchi survives that one too.

193
00:06:21,756 --> 00:06:23,256
He lives to be ninety-three.

194
00:06:23,767 --> 00:06:26,977
By evening, Hiroshima has stopped burning, mostly

195
00:06:26,987 --> 00:06:28,937
because there is nothing left to burn.

196
00:06:29,448 --> 00:06:32,250
From the hills, the city glows red through

197
00:06:32,260 --> 00:06:32,898
the night.

198
00:06:33,389 --> 00:06:36,127
Schools and temples on the outskirts fill with

199
00:06:36,137 --> 00:06:36,787
the injured.

200
00:06:37,062 --> 00:06:38,600
There are almost no doctors.

201
00:06:38,862 --> 00:06:40,650
Most of the city's hospitals are gone.

202
00:06:41,161 --> 00:06:43,164
And then something begins that no one can

203
00:06:43,174 --> 00:06:43,911
explain.

204
00:06:44,174 --> 00:06:46,924
People who seemed unhurt start to fall ill.

205
00:06:47,249 --> 00:06:47,761
Fever.

206
00:06:48,024 --> 00:06:48,724
Weakness.

207
00:06:48,986 --> 00:06:50,874
Their hair comes out in their hands.

208
00:06:51,353 --> 00:06:53,550
No one in Hiroshima has ever heard the

209
00:06:53,560 --> 00:06:54,610
word radiation.

210
00:06:54,860 --> 00:06:57,125
Doctors write down what they see, and call

211
00:06:57,135 --> 00:06:58,722
it atomic bomb disease.

212
00:06:59,196 --> 00:07:01,962
And yet, three days after the bomb, on

213
00:07:01,972 --> 00:07:04,337
August ninth, a sound comes back to the

214
00:07:04,347 --> 00:07:05,309
ruined city.

215
00:07:05,813 --> 00:07:06,672
A streetcar.

216
00:07:06,934 --> 00:07:09,997
One short line, running again through the ashes.

217
00:07:10,259 --> 00:07:12,422
Driven by the same teenage girls.

218
00:07:12,908 --> 00:07:14,886
People stop in the rubble and stare.

219
00:07:15,149 --> 00:07:16,049
Some of them cry.

220
00:07:16,311 --> 00:07:17,474
Something still works.

221
00:07:17,967 --> 00:07:19,819
Today, a modern city of more than a

222
00:07:19,829 --> 00:07:23,179
million people stands on the same seven rivers.

223
00:07:23,652 --> 00:07:25,957
But at its center, one ruin has been

224
00:07:25,967 --> 00:07:27,792
left exactly as it was.

225
00:07:28,055 --> 00:07:29,680
A hall with a skeleton dome.

226
00:07:29,942 --> 00:07:32,882
The bomb burst almost directly above it, and

227
00:07:32,892 --> 00:07:34,630
so its walls stayed standing.

228
00:07:35,115 --> 00:07:37,601
In the museum nearby, there's a wristwatch.

229
00:07:37,839 --> 00:07:39,639
Its hands stopped at eight fifteen.

230
00:07:40,162 --> 00:07:41,411
And a set of stone steps from the

231
00:07:41,421 --> 00:07:43,324
front of a bank, with a dark shape

232
00:07:43,334 --> 00:07:43,871
on them.

233
00:07:44,134 --> 00:07:46,259
The shadow of someone who was sitting there,

234
00:07:46,559 --> 00:07:48,034
waiting for the doors to open.

235
00:07:48,539 --> 00:07:50,597
Nobody knows the exact toll.

236
00:07:50,872 --> 00:07:53,324
The most careful estimates say between ninety

237
00:07:53,334 --> 00:07:56,037
thousand and one hundred and sixty-six thousand

238
00:07:56,047 --> 00:07:58,024
people had died by the end of that

239
00:07:58,034 --> 00:07:58,547
year.

240
00:07:59,020 --> 00:08:02,193
Of the nine thousand students tearing down houses

241
00:08:02,203 --> 00:08:05,815
that morning, nearly two thirds never came home.

242
00:08:06,320 --> 00:08:06,785
So.

243
00:08:07,035 --> 00:08:08,122
Would you have survived?

244
00:08:08,616 --> 00:08:11,208
It was never about being brave, or smart,

245
00:08:11,270 --> 00:08:12,158
or fast.

246
00:08:12,420 --> 00:08:14,195
It was about where you happened to be

247
00:08:14,333 --> 00:08:16,245
standing at eight fifteen.

248
00:08:16,758 --> 00:08:19,328
Outdoors, within a kilometer of the center,

249
00:08:19,578 --> 00:08:21,090
almost no one lived.

250
00:08:21,581 --> 00:08:24,302
Behind thick concrete, your chances rose.

251
00:08:24,540 --> 00:08:27,092
A handful of people survived within five hundred

252
00:08:27,102 --> 00:08:30,830
meters, nearly all of them inside heavy buildings

253
00:08:30,840 --> 00:08:31,790
or basements.

254
00:08:32,301 --> 00:08:35,191
Three kilometers out, like Yamaguchi, most people

255
00:08:35,201 --> 00:08:37,316
lived, if they moved away from the fires

256
00:08:37,326 --> 00:08:38,489
and didn't drink the rain.

257
00:08:38,987 --> 00:08:40,628
And one man lived at a hundred and

258
00:08:40,638 --> 00:08:42,788
seventy meters, because at the right moment,

259
00:08:42,900 --> 00:08:45,375
he went downstairs to look for some papers.

260
00:08:45,844 --> 00:08:48,405
He lived until nineteen eighty-two, and spent

261
00:08:48,415 --> 00:08:50,830
years writing down the names of the thirty-six

262
00:08:50,840 --> 00:08:51,478
who didn't.

263
00:08:51,969 --> 00:08:53,479
Where would you have been standing that morning?

264
00:08:53,741 --> 00:08:54,904
Tell me in the comments.

